<!--
  Seikilos
  The Seikilos epitaph: the oldest surviving complete musical composition.
  Source: M. L. West, Ancient Greek Music (Oxford 1992), no. 15, p. 301, in
  the transcription a semitone above the stone's pitch (tonic f#). The stele
  is Copenhagen, National Museum inv. 14897, 1st c. AD.

  Hoson zes, phainou, / meden holos sy lypou; /
  pros oligon esti to zen, / to telos ho chronos apaitei.
  "While you live, shine; have no grief at all; life exists only for a short
  while, and time demands its due."

  The stone gives pitch and rhythm only. Our realisation choices: the tempo
  (a dotted quarter at 66; the song tempo counts quarters, so 99), the
  octave doubling and the drone.

  The scale is the Mixolydian mode on C of the Pythagorean gamut, C D E F G A
  B-flat = 1/1 9/8 81/64 4/3 3/2 27/16 16/9. In 53-EDO's notation the plain
  names are the just major scale, so the Pythagorean E, A and B-flat are
  written E-up, A-up and B-flat-down.
-->
